    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Chaucer set a collection of tales inside a pilgrimage, a framework no earlier work is known to have used, and let the pilgrims' classes, occupations and views shape the stories they tell.</li><li>He planned about 120 stories, four from each of some 30 pilgrims, but most scholars think the work was incomplete at the end of his life, and there is no consensus on its intended order.</li><li>No <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in Chaucer's hand survives; the text comes from 84 manuscripts and four incunabula, and later writers added tales and prologues to fill its gaps.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Canterbury Tales survives in more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> than any other vernacular English literary text except Prick of Conscience, yet Derek Pearsall cautions that this should not be taken as proof of its popularity in the century after Chaucer's death.</p></aside>
    </section>

